    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d754">

                <pb facs="0849" n="839" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg839"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.6336/11–1848: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Consul General at Mukden</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Ward</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-11-22T12:00:00-05:00">November 22,
                            1948—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>216. Dept judges your unnumbered tel 4 p. m. 18th<anchor n="56" xml:id="d754-f1"
                    /> Communists have seized and taken physical possession your radio transmitter
                    and notes essential difference between ordering you cease use radio and outright
                    seizure US Govt property. Dept takes serious view latter action and suggests
                    unless you consider such action unwise you protest vigorously this or any other
                    seizure US Govt property, keeping in mind Deptel 187 Nov 2.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lovett</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d754-f1" n="56" xml:id="d754-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 2464, November 18, from the Consul General at Shanghai, <ref
                        target="#pg837">p. 837</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
